But somehow it has become the industrial
standard for cleaning web crawl data sets.

00:19:56.240 --> 00:20:00.799
Again, those data set builders bet
almost the whole public web's porn

00:20:00.799 --> 00:20:05.759
content on this one-person single
-handedly maintained bookmark-like list.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:08.480
Okay.

00:20:08.599 --> 00:20:11.579
The last example of filtering
with block lists is not

00:20:11.579 --> 00:20:16.859
exactly a block list, but it's
a quite interesting choice.

00:20:17.339 --> 00:20:23.900
The Pile, which was used to train a
lot of major models several years ago.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:27.259
It has been taken down because it includes Books3,

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:32.220
which includes a lot of
books from shadow libraries.

00:20:32.759 --> 00:20:36.740
The Pile decided not to include
three data sets in particular.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:42.980
The first one, short fan fictions
from AO3 and Fanfiction.net.

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:48.619
The second one, the literotica, a website
hosting erotic stories and audience.

00:20:49.279 --> 00:20:51.880
And the third one, US congressional record.

00:20:58.829 --> 00:21:05.450
So, filtering with classifiers is another
popular option to remove dirty data.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:09.869
A lot of data sets don't try to gloss it over and

00:21:09.869 --> 00:21:12.630
don't mention what exactly detector they used.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:20.690
And a lot of data sets basically come
up with not safe for work detectors.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.130
But this time we don't have time to go into this.

00:21:24.269 --> 00:21:25.869
It can be another chapter by itself.

00:21:27.069 --> 00:21:32.150
The Pile and the RefinedWeb use similar
detectors trained from Wikipedia comments.

00:21:32.369 --> 00:21:35.549
Wikipedia has been the guy,
like, when it comes to good

00:21:35.549 --> 00:21:38.509
English or toxic English,
they always go to Wikipedia.

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:45.190
But the Wikipedia comments trained this
profanity checker. It's built for English.

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:49.410
And then The Pile noticed that
whenever there's a German text

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:55.789
popping up, the definite article
"die" is always flagged as profane.

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:05.869
And then MassiveText from
DeepMind, or basically Google, used

00:22:05.869 --> 00:22:10.230
to train Gopher, it's a very
often referred heuristic source.

00:22:10.549 --> 00:22:13.829
They didn't even bother to
find a classifier to use.

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:15.849
They just used Google safe search filter.

00:22:16.549 --> 00:22:20.509
They described that safe search
incorporates various web signals to

00:22:20.509 --> 00:22:27.390
identify explicit content with reasons
that a block list like C4 used could

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:32.289
disproportionately filter out inoffensive
content associated with minority groups.

00:22:32.529 --> 00:22:36.869
Which is very true. But is
Google safe search any better?

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:43.789
In Monea's paper, I know it when I see it,
the heteronormativity of Google safe search.

00:22:44.009 --> 00:22:46.210
She described Google safe search as straight.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:49.789
It started as a text-based
pornographic detector and

00:22:49.789 --> 00:22:52.390
then later it was trained on WordNet and ImageNet.

00:22:52.549 --> 00:22:57.410
If you still remember this critical
reflection from seven years ago.

00:22:58.690 --> 00:23:02.490
So, before I can decide if
Google safe search or the naughty

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:07.309
list is worse, new open data set
has dropped, and they are

00:23:07.309 --> 00:23:11.250
already using LLMs to rate
the toxicity of training data.

00:23:11.769 --> 00:23:18.890
And then try to train a classifier from
this LLM generated whatever annotation.

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:24.130
And then use this classifier to clean
the data for training another LLM.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:27.390
They will just never stop finding ways to build

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:29.970
up unopenable black box on top of each other.

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:35.789
So, it's an extra part.

00:23:36.549 --> 00:23:40.869
A saying something but at the same
time saying nothing style heuristics.

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:45.390
Wikipedia-based text, image data sets.

00:23:45.569 --> 00:23:50.170
Also, again, from Google, they seemed not
quite sure what they needed to remove.

00:23:50.549 --> 00:23:56.470
So, they were aiming for remove "pornographic,
profane, violent, and dot, dot, dot content."

00:23:56.470 --> 00:24:00.009
But even though they don't know
what they're talking about anymore,

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:04.730
they still managed it with their
"multiple techniques based on

00:24:04.730 --> 00:24:08.769
sophisticated image understanding,
multilingual understanding models."

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:16.710
All right. And WuDao, it was once
the largest Chinese text corpora.

00:24:17.069 --> 00:24:19.609
I was thinking about referring
to the newer ones, but the

00:24:19.609 --> 00:24:22.490
newer ones was just filtering
with keywords block lists.

00:24:23.009 --> 00:24:28.690
So, I just sticked with it, at least it
was somewhat politically honest.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:32.250
So, WuDao removed dirty words.

00:24:32.609 --> 00:24:37.289
This we know, more than 50 words are
dirty words from Mandarin Web, right?

00:24:37.849 --> 00:24:45.490
So, and then they also tried to remove
seditious comments and other illegal comments

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:50.490
has adverse effects on building a
harmonious and positive social environment.

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:56.490
Does this sound familiar to you?
Definitely sounds very familiar to me.

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:01.670
And two years later, in 2023,
Beijing released the interim measure

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:06.230
that requires artificial
intelligence must demonstrate core

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:10.769
socialist value and have to be in
line with CCP's political ideology.

00:25:11.089 --> 00:25:15.569
But in this case, only working with training
data is not effective for censoring.

00:25:15.769 --> 00:25:19.710
Most efforts are in the reinforcement
learning from human feedback after training.

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:25.950
I mentioned that I kind of worked on
this project a bit more this year.

00:25:26.069 --> 00:25:28.730
There's another aspect I'm
working on at the moment.

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:33.170
Now that we have no way to know
how large language models from big

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:38.430
companies are trained from their data
sets or how their data set is cleaned.

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:44.109
But it would be surprising if they don't
refer to any existing cleaning methods, right?

00:25:44.109 --> 00:25:49.690
And if we partially know how they filter
the data set, doesn't that mean that

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:55.049
we can follow those rigid estimations and
make our data too dirty to be trained.

00:25:55.890 --> 00:26:00.589
Or if we know how unwanted data
look like, can we develop our own

00:26:00.589 --> 00:26:05.710
heuristics to make our data look
similar to that and create an opt-out

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:09.990
method for ourselves when it's not
possible to get it from big techs.

00:26:10.529 --> 00:26:14.569
So I put these heuristics
online and developed a set

00:26:14.569 --> 00:26:17.509
of anti-heuristic heuristics
to make our data dirty.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:17.509
